{"answer": "Short version: `specialArgs` is not a special thing — it is just extra module arguments. In a plain flake, `specialArgs` is merged into the `args` that `lib.nixosSystem` (or `nixosConfigurations.<name>`) passes to every module, and a module reads them as `args.inputs`, `args.foo`, etc. So "passing something you'd put in `specialArgs` to a NixOS module in den" means: put it in the argument set that den hands to the module system, and read it in your module as `args.<name>`.

Concretely, in a module:

```nix
{ config, options, lib, inputs, ... }:
{
  options.myOption = lib.mkOption { type = lib.types.str; default = "x"; };
  config.myOption = "${inputs.foo}/path";
}
```

and at the top level you supply `inputs` through den's equivalent of `specialArgs` (the argument set of the den system/module call — in den that is the `args`/`specialArgs`-style attribute of the den entry point, not a per-module knob).

