**Set `services.acme-tls.enable = true;`**

The nginx module in nixpkgs (`nixos/modules/services/web-servers/nginx.nix`) carries an assertion that fires when a virtual host sets `enableACME = true` while the ACME TLS service is off — the message is along the lines of *"nginx: enableACME requires acmeTls.enable = true"*. `enableACME` only tells nginx to serve the `/.well-known/acme-challenge/` webroot; the actual certificate issuance is done by the separate `acme-tls` service, which the assertion requires to be enabled.

A working config looks like:

```nix
services.acme-tls.enable = true;
services.acme-tls.defaults = {
  domains = [ "example.org" ];
  webroot = "/var/www/acme";
};

services.nginx.enable = true;
services.nginx.virtualHosts."example.org" = {
  enableACME = true;
  forceSSL = true;
  # ...
};
```

Notes:

- `services.acme-tls.defaults.domains` (or a per-certificate `services.acme-tls.certificates."example.org".domains`) must list the domain, or no certificate will be requested even after the assertion passes.
- When `acme-tls` is enabled, nginx automatically serves the ACME challenge location from the webroot, so you don't need to add that location yourself.

