## Recommended: a specialisation

For "same host, one option changed" (like a GPU driver), NixOS has a built-in mechanism: `nixos.specialisation.<name>`. It's built as part of your main system and gets its own boot entry (a GRUB submenu under your system, or a separate entry under systemd-boot).

In your flake:

```nix
nixosConfigurations.myhost = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
  system = "x86_64-linux";
  modules = [
    ./hosts/myhost.nix
    {
      nixos.specialisation.nvidia = {
        config = {
          hardware.nvidia.enable = true;
          hardware.nvidia.modesetting.enable = true;
          # e.g. switch display manager if needed
        };
      };
    }
  ];
};
```

Then just `nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#myhost` once. At boot, GRUB shows your system with a submenu entry for the specialisation, so you pick the driver variant per boot without maintaining two full systems.

Notes:
- A specialisation is evaluated **on top of** your base config. If your base has `hardware.nvidia.enable = true` and you want an amdgpu variant, the specialisation must explicitly set `hardware.nvidia.enable = false` and enable the amdgpu bits.
- Kernel modules for the specialisation are built into it, so driver-only differences work cleanly.

## Alternative: two full systems

If the variants diverge more, define two `nixosSystem`s in the flake:

```nix
nixosConfigurations.myhost-amdgpu = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
  system = "x86_64-linux";
  modules = [ ./hosts/myhost.nix { hardware.nvidia.enable = false; } ];
};
```

Install each with `nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#myhost-amdgpu` (and `.#myhost`). Each `switch` registers a boot entry; set `nixos.system.name` in each (e.g. `"myhost (amdgpu)"`) so the entries are distinguishable in the menu.

Both approaches work with `boot.loader.grub.enable = true` or `boot.loader.systemd-boot.enable = true`.

