## Forbid root SSH logins on NixOS

Set `PermitRootLogin` to `"no"` in `services.openssh.settings`:

```nix
{
  services.openssh = {
    enable = true;
    settings = {
      PermitRootLogin = "no";
    };
  };
}
```

Then `nixos-rebuild switch` (or `switch --flake .#host`).

### What the values mean
`PermitRootLogin` is a passthrough to the `sshd` option of the same name:

- `"no"` — root **cannot** log in over SSH at all (this is what you want).
- `"prohibit-password"` — root can log in **only** with a public key (this is the NixOS default).
- `"without-password"` — older alias for `prohibit-password`.
- `"yes"` — root can log in with a password (least secure).

So if you only want to stop *password* root logins, `"prohibit-password"` (the default) already does that; to forbid root SSH logins entirely, use `"no"`.

### Caveats
- This only affects SSH. Root is still reachable via the console/TTY and `sudo`/`su` from a normal user.
- If you rely on root SSH for anything (e.g. a management path), make sure you have another way in before switching, so you don't lock yourself out.

I could not verify the exact option name/default against the nixpkgs source in this run (tools unavailable), so treat the `"prohibit-password"` default claim as unconfirmed here.

