I tried to verify this against den's source, but in this environment `clone`, `web_fetch`, `web_search`, `eval` and `recall` were all unavailable, so I could not read the actual aspect/capability API. Here is my best-effort answer, flagged as unverified.

## How capability-gated aspects work in den

The pattern you want is: **an aspect declares the capabilities it needs, and den only applies that aspect (and its homeManager config) to hosts that declare those capabilities.** You do not hand-write an `if host.capabilities ? graphics` guard in every module — the capability check is part of the aspect declaration.

Sketch of the two sides:

**Host side** — each host declares its capabilities:

```nix
{
  den.hosts.laptop = {
    capabilities = [ "graphics" "battery" ];
    # ...
  };
  den.hosts.server = {
    capabilities = [ ];
    # ...
  };
}
```

**Feature/aspect side** — the aspect lists the capabilities it requires, and carries the homeManager config:

```nix
{
  name = "my-graphics-feature";
  capabilities = [ "graphics" ];   # only applied where the host has this
  module = {
    home-manager.users.alice = {
      # your homeManager config here
    };
  };
}
```

den then matches each host's `capabilities` against each aspect's required `capabilities`, and only composes the aspect's module into hosts that satisfy it. So `laptop` (has `graphics`) gets the homeManager config; `server` (no `graphics`) does not.

## What I could not verify

- The exact field names (`capabilities` on the aspect, and how the aspect is registered/applied by name) — I could not read den's source in this environment.
- Whether the capability match is "aspect requires a subset of the host's capabilities" (my assumption) versus some other semantics.
- The exact shape of the homeManager hook inside an aspect module.

If you can share the den repo/rev you're on (or confirm the repo is `den-org/den`), I can re-run this with `clone`/`eval` and confirm the exact API and cite the file/line.

