## The pattern

den models each host as a NixOS configuration and exposes them as top-level flake outputs under `nixosConfigurations.<host>`. deploy-rs, in turn, reads `deploy.nodes.<name>` and deploys the `nixosConfigurations.<name>` that matches by name. So you don't need den to emit `deploy.nodes` directly — you generate `deploy.nodes` yourself in your flake's `outputs`, keyed by the same host names den uses, and fill each entry with the SSH connection details.

```nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-24.05";
    den.url     = "github:numtide/den";
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, den }:
    let
      # den builds one nixosConfigurations.<host> per host you define
      hosts = den.lib.nixosConfigurations {
        nixpkgs = nixpkgs;
        # ... your den host definitions ...
      };

      # connection info per host, keyed by the same names
      conn = {
        web1 = { host = "web1.example.com"; user = "deploy"; sshKey = ./secrets/web1_ed25519; };
        db1  = { host = "db1.example.com";  user = "deploy"; sshKey = ./secrets/db1_ed25519;  };
      };
    in {
      nixosConfigurations = hosts;

      deploy.nodes = mapAttrs (name: c: {
        host     = c.host;
        user     = c.user;
        sshKey   = c.sshKey;
        # port = 22;
        # targetHost = ...;
        # extraNixosConfiguration = ...;
      }) conn;
    };
}
```

Then `deploy-rs deploy web1` (or `deploy-rs deploy --all`) picks up `deploy.nodes.web1` and deploys `nixosConfigurations.web1`.

