All my verification tools (`recall`, `clone`, `web_fetch`, `eval`) are unavailable in this evaluation, so I'm answering from general NixOS knowledge and flagging what I couldn't check below.

## The core idea

In NixOS, **one `nixosConfigurations.<name>` = one boot entry**. The boot loader (systemd-boot or GRUB) gets one menu entry per configuration, labeled from `nixos.systemName` + `nixos.version`. So to get a "same host, different GPU driver" variant you pick at boot, you define **two configurations that share one base config and differ only in the GPU driver options**. Each install adds its own entry to the same ESP, and you choose at boot.

## Concrete flake

```nix
{
  description = "My host, GPU-driver variants";

  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-24.05";

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }:
    let
      system = "x86_64-linux";
      base = [ ./common.nix ];   # shared: fileSystems, users, hostname, bootloader, etc.
    in {
      nixosConfigurations = {
        host-nvidia = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
          inherit system;
          modules = base ++ [ ({ ... }: {
            nixos.systemName = "host (NVIDIA)";   # must differ from the other
            nixpkgs.config.allowUnfree = true;    # nvidia is non-free
            hardware.nvidia.enable = true;
            hardware.nvidia.modesetting.enable = true;
            services.xserver.videoDrivers = [ "nvidia" ];
          })];
        };

        host-amdgpu = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
          inherit system;
          modules = base ++ [ ({ ... }: {
            nixos.systemName = "host (AMD)";
            hardware.nvidia.enable = false;
            services.xserver.videoDrivers = [ "amdgpu" ];
          })];
        };
      };
    };
}
```

`common.nix` holds everything both variants share — critically the **same root filesystem** (`fileSystems."/"` device) and the **same boot loader** (`boot.loader.systemd-boot.enable = true` or `boot.loader.grub.enable = true`), so both entries land in the same ESP.

## Install both

```sh
nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#host-nvidia
nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#host-amdgpu
```

Each `switch` writes its own entry into `/boot/loader/entries/` (systemd-boot) or the GRUB menu. At boot you pick "host (NVIDIA)" or "host (AMD)".

## Gotchas

- **`nixos.systemName` must differ** between the two, or the menu shows two identical labels and you can't tell them apart.
- **NVIDIA needs `allowUnfree = true`** (and the non-free kernel module) in that variant only.
- **Same boot loader + same ESP** for both, or the second install won't coexist cleanly with the first.
- **Same root device** in both — they're variants of one host, not two disks.
- For **Wayland**, the kernel driver (amdgpu / i915 / nvidia) is what matters; the compositor picks it up. The `services.xserver.videoDrivers` line is the X11 path.

